Precedent P-13: The Abbot and the Press
(Sponheim and Mainz, 1492 to 1494)
Forty years after Gutenberg, the abbot Johannes Trithemius wrote De
Laude Scriptorum, “In Praise of Scribes.” Hand-copying, he argued,
was  devotional  work  worth  keeping,  and  he  doubted  that  paper
would last as long as parchment. He also saw that a printer would
never bother with plenty of texts that still deserved to survive.  His-
tory of Information, Trithemius
Then he wanted people to actually read his argument. So he had it
printed: Peter Friedbergʼs press in Mainz, 1494. Paderborn University
Library, 1494 printed edition
The  legend  calls  that  hypocrisy.  Itʼs  something  more  useful.
Trithemius  didnʼt  think  printing  was  evil;  he  thought  it  didnʼt  do
everything, and he used it for the job it did well. And the old skills
werenʼt gone either. Forty years earlier, in August 1456, a craftsman
named Heinrich Cremer wrote his name into a paper copy of the
Gutenberg Bible now in Franceʼs national library, recording that heʼd
done  its  illumination,  rubrication,  and  binding.  Bibliothèque
nationale  de  France,  Gutenberg  Bible The  press  made  the  text.  A
human  hand  still  finished  the  book.  The  unit  of  change  is  often
smaller than the whole occupation: one task moves, another stays,
and the terms change for the people doing them.
